.TH help "" "Command"
.SH NAME
help \- print a brief description of a command's usage
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBhelp [\fIcommand ...\fB]\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
help -- print concise command description
Usage:	help [ command ... ]
help is intended for refresher information.
To learn about a new command, man or learn is preferable.
If 'command' is omitted, prints information about $LASTERROR.
.HE
.B help
prints a brief description of the usage of each
.IR command .
If
.I command
is omitted,
.B help
instead prints a short note about itself and then, if the
environment variable
.B $LASTERROR
is set, describes the command named there.
If
.I command
is omitted and
.B $LASTERROR
is not set,
.B help
exits without printing anything further.
.PP
For each
.IR command ,
.B help
first looks for the file
.B /usr/man/cmd/\fIcommand\fR
or, failing that,
.BR /usr/man/cmd/\fIcommand\fBm\fR ,
and, if either exists, prints the text found between its
.B .HS
and
.B .HE
lines.
.PP
Failing that,
.B help
looks for a line
.B #\fIcommand\fR
in the system file
.B /etc/helpfile
and, if the environment variable
.B $HELP
names a file, in that file as well, and prints the lines that
follow it up to the next line beginning with
.BR # .
To speed up repeated lookups in a large
.BR /etc/helpfile ,
.B help
maintains an index file,
.BR /etc/helpindex ,
which it rebuilds whenever
.B /etc/helpfile
is newer than the index.
.SH FILES
.DS
.ta 20n
\fB/usr/man/cmd/\fIcommand\fR	preformatted manual page for \fIcommand\fR
\fB/etc/helpfile\fR	system command summaries
\fB/etc/helpindex\fR	index for \fB/etc/helpfile\fR
\fB$HELP\fR	user's own command summaries, if set
\fB$LASTERROR\fR	command described when none is given
.DE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.B man
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B help
prints
.B "No information on %s"
for any
.I command
it cannot find a description for, and exits with a nonzero status
if any
.I command
could not be described.
